! mcp_bootstrap_p162.strap -- the SPACE pseudo-op (R 1 / R 2) and the
! SPACE LABEL entry (RS1 FLK), printed page 162.
! docs/MCP_Mar63_001-249.pdf PDF p.169, header READ and not assumed:
! the top line prints "TIME CLOCK 00054041  PUNID 11D11MCP   CARD ID
! IH000001   DIST. OF 3/01   3/01   PAGE NUMBER  162", and the
! column-heading row's own "LOCATION" field is BLANK.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE HEADING-ROW LOCATION FIELD IS BLANK, AND THAT IS CORRECT
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! docs/transcription_workflow.md 1 says the field is LINE 1'S
! location and is blank when line 1 compiles nothing.  Line 1 here
! is an asterisk banner, so BLANK is the predicted reading and a
! blank is NOT a zero.  Rendered at 400 dpi the field is bare paper
! with the dashed rule running through it; no digits were invented
! from it.  The page's origin comes from line 4 instead.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THE PAGE IS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! FORTY-EIGHT PRINTED LINES, THIRTY-NINE OF WHICH COMPILE BITS.
! Lines 1-3 are the SPACE banner, line 4 is "SLC,$", lines 38-42 are
! a blank comment card, the SPACE LABEL banner and the two-line
! CALLING SEQUENCE comment, and line 42 is blank again.  Everything
! else is an instruction: thirty-two half words and seven full
! words, 23 words (0o27) in all, then blank paper to the foot.
!
! Two entry points:
!
!   lines  5-11  R 1, the SPACE pseudo-op's four-way dispatch on
!                what the problem program asked for.  Four
!                LVI,$0,<code> / B,R 2 pairs, one per control-op
!                code: 62 (SPACE BLOCK), 126 (BACKSPACE BLOCK), 63
!                (SPACE FILE) and 127 -- the comment column names
!                the first three and the fourth (127) falls through
!                into line 12 with no comment.  THREE OF THE FOUR
!                BRANCHES ARE IDENTICAL WORDS (220124.10 00), which
!                is a free internal check on R 2's address.
!
!   lines 12-37  R 2, the common body.  It sets the return address
!                (V-I,2,.32 -- "-SET RETURN ADDRESS"), marks the
!                channel busy (CM1111 on S CH OP), saves the IC in
!                S TIC, tests that the unit is a tape (KVI,4,4.0 /
!                BZXE,K ILEGL -- "-TEST IF EQUIP IS TAPE" / "-IF
!                NOT, FAKE EPGK"), loads R MASK and the control-op
!                code into the scratch pad, and goes round the
!                status-evaluation call (SIC,S EVL TB($12) ;
!                B,Z STEVL) and into K K LOC and R VFY.  Lines 24-28
!                are the FILE-COUNT-ZERO test: LV,5,S FILEK, then
!                BXVGZ to R 9 SKP if the count is positive,
!                otherwise BB on S CTL OP+.1 to K ILEGL ("-BSP OR
!                REW AT LABEL ** EPGK") and a call to RS1 FLK.
!                Lines 29-37 build and issue the CTL: the op code
!                goes to the right effect address (SVA,1,R10.32 --
!                "-CTL OP CODE TO RT EFFECT ADDR"), the CTL's own
!                address word is planted by LX,0,$+1.0 / SX,0,S IO
!                INS($12), and after R10's CTL the control bit is
!                set and control leaves through K SUPP / M IOREJ.
!
!   lines 43-48  RS1 FLK, the SPACE LABEL entry, whose calling
!                sequence lines 40-41 print in full:
!                "LV,5,S FILE K($12)  (FILE K +0 OR -0)" and
!                "SIC,S SCRPM($12) + B,RS1 FLK".  It tests the
!                system-tape flag (BZB,S SYSTM($12),K N SYST), sets
!                the control bit, branches on the sign of index
!                register 5's count field (BZB,$5.24,K SP TM --
!                "-BRANCH IF FILE COUNT IS +0"), and otherwise
!                clears S INST D ("-IF FILE COUNT -0") and leaves
!                through K DENST ("-SET UNIT TO INSTALAATION DEN",
!                IBM's own misspelling, reproduced).
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! FORTY-EIGHT LINES, FORTY CARDS, AND NOTHING FELL OFF EITHER END
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE SURVEY SAID 27 ROWS, 220120.40..220142.40.  It is short by
! TWELVE rows and it lost the whole tail: the page's last located
! row is 220147.00 (line 48), not 220142.40.  It got the first row
! right.
!
! Counted off the CARD-SERIAL column at 400 dpi the page runs
! IH000001 (line 1) to IH000040 (line 48), CONSECUTIVE, with EIGHT
! serials printed twice -- one card, two statements:
!
!   IH000005  lines  5,6    LVI,$0,62.0      ; B,R 2
!   IH000006  lines  7,8    LVI,$0,126.0     ; B,R 2
!   IH000007  lines  9,10   LVI,$0,63.0      ; B,R 2
!   IH000017  lines 20,21   SIC,S EVL TB($12); B,Z STEVL
!   IH000023  lines 27,28   SIC,S SCR PM($12); B,RS1 FLK
!   IH000027  lines 32,33   LX,0,$+1.0       ; SX,0,S IO INS($12)
!   IH000030  lines 36,37   LVI,1,K SUPP     ; B,M IOREJ
!   IH000040  lines 47,48   SIC,S RET AD($12); B,K DENST
!
! 48 - 8 = 40 cards, which is exactly the serial range.  Every one
! of the eight is a load/branch or SIC/branch pair, so the doubling
! is the programmer keeping a pair on one card -- and the page shows
! the SAME convention spelled with the continuation character in its
! own comment, line 41's "SIC,S SCRPM($12) + B,RS1 FLK".
!
! THE TOP is closed three ways.  (1) Line 1's serial IH000001 IS the
! header's own "CARD ID IH000001", so no row is above line 1 -- and
! this page STARTS A NEW CARD SECTION (printed p.160's serials are
! IE0000nn), so the serial 1 is not merely consecutive, it is the
! first card of the deck.  (2) Line 4 prints "220120.40  *  SLC,$",
! and an SLC emits nothing, so its printed LOCATION _is_ the counter
! arriving from printed p.161.  (3) The blank heading-row LOCATION
! field is what the rule predicts for a banner line 1.
!
! THE BOTTOM is closed by arithmetic that does not depend on reading
! blank paper: 32 half-word rows + 7 full-word rows = 46 half words
! = 23 words = 0o27, and 220120.40 + 0o27 words = 220147.40.  The
! deck's assembled counter arrives there.  See THE JOIN FORWARD.
!
! >>> AND ONE CARD SERIAL IS MISSING ACROSS THE PAGE BREAK: IH000041
! <<<  Measured at 900 dpi on BOTH pages, not inferred.  This page's
! last two lines (47 and 48) share IH000040, and printed p.163's
! first five lines read IH000042, IH000042, IH000043, IH000044,
! IH000044 -- so IH000041 is printed on neither page.  It is NOT a
! lost row of this page: a STRAP listing prints a line for every
! card, including comment cards, and the two pages' addresses are
! contiguous (line 48 is a HALF word at 220147.00, which leaves
! 220147.40, and p.163's line 1 is at 220147.40).  A card that
! compiled anything would have to have shown up in that gap and
! there is no gap.  The best guess is a listing-control card that
! forces the page eject -- this page stops at line 48 with room
! below, where printed p.159 runs to line 50 -- but THAT IS A GUESS
! AND THIS DECK DOES NOT ASSERT IT.  Recorded in WHAT COULD NOT BE
! RESOLVED.
!
! THERE IS NO HOLE IN THE IMAGE.  No DR/DRZ reservation and no
! forward SLC on this page; the only SLC is line 4's, at the word
! the previous page left.  Lines 38-42 compile nothing, but they do
! not move the counter either: line 37 ends at 220143.00 and line 43
! begins at 220143.00.  The span 220120.40..220147.40 is dense.
!
! AND THE STRADDLED WORD WAS MEASURED, not assumed.  WORD 220120 IS
! WRITTEN BY TWO PAGES -- printed p.161's line 28 at 220120.00 and
! this page's line 5 at 220120.40 -- so a merge that ASSIGNED rather
! than read-modify-wrote would silently zero one half, and a zero
! half word is BRANCH ENABLED TO LOCATION ZERO.  Run with p.161's
! deck beside this one through mcp_image.merge():
!
!   word 220120 = 220017.50 00 | 000076.01 01
!
! both halves present, and the merged span 220106..220147 has ZERO
! holes.  That is the strongest form of "the span is dense" available
! and it is the one thing verify_mcp_page cannot see.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! GLYPHS -- WHAT WAS SETTLED, AND ON WHAT
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! This page opens round glyphs constantly: "220120.4C" for
! 220120.40, "COCC76.C1 C1" for 000076.01 01, "COCCCO.45 CD" for
! 000000.45 0D, "22C064.32 CO" for 220064.32 C0.  Every round glyph
! in a LOCATION field or in one of the two OCTAL fields of a half
! word is a 0 BY CONSTRUCTION -- those fields can only hold octal
! digits (docs/transcription_workflow.md 2b rung 5).  That sets the
! page as one that drops ink on round glyphs; it does NOT license
! converting any particular C elsewhere.
!
! (1) EVERY DOUBTFUL GLYPH IN A MNEMONIC OR OPERAND WAS SETTLED BY
!     WHAT PRODUCED THE BITS, not by ink.  All thirty-nine rows
!     reproduce the printed BINARY OUTPUT character for character on
!     the first assembly, so the statement column is pinned by the
!     binary column.  Where a reading was genuinely in doubt, BOTH
!     were handed to strap_asm and the wrong one is not a program
!     (docs/transcription_workflow.md 2b rung 3).  Measured:
!
!       BXVGZ,R 9 SKP   assembles, and prints the page's own C2 byte
!       BXVCZ,R 9 SKP   "unknown mnemonic 'BXVCZ'"
!       BXVOZ,R 9 SKP   "unknown mnemonic 'BXVOZ'"
!
!     so line 25's third-from-last glyph is a G.  (XVGZ is indicator
!     51 in strap/opcodes.py's INDICATOR_BITS, "index value greater
!     than zero", which is also what line 25's comment "-TEST FOR
!     FILE K ZERO" describes.)  The same test eliminates the C
!     reading of the register field on lines 18, 32, 33 and 34:
!
!       SV,C,S CTL OP($12)   "undefined symbol 'C'"
!       LX,C,$+1.0           "undefined symbol 'C'"
!       SX,C,S IO INS($12)   "undefined symbol 'C'"
!       CTL,C.C($14),C.0     "undefined symbol 'C'"
!       LVI,$O,62.0          "unsupported system symbol $'O'"
!
!     Each is printed with an open round glyph on the page and each
!     is a 0.  This is an ELIMINATOR and not a confirmation: it
!     fires here only because no deck in this repository defines a
!     symbol named C.
!
! (2) THE SAME STATEMENT PRINTED THREE TIMES, rung 4 and the
!     strongest one.  Lines 5, 7 and 9 are "LVI,$0,<n>.0" in the
!     same character positions off the same drum.  Line 5 prints its
!     zero OPEN ("$C") and lines 7 and 9 print it CLOSED ("$0"), so
!     the page itself shows one character varying with the ink while
!     everything around it is fixed.  Lines 35 and 44 repeat the
!     trick with "CM1111(BU,1),S CTL($11)", which compiles the same
!     word twice (000000.74 8B  001000.36 F0).
!
! (3) DIGIT 1 vs LETTER I IN THE PAGE'S OWN LABELS, settled at 1800
!     dpi against TWO known glyphs in the same column band.  This
!     matters because R 1, R 2, R 9 SKP and R10 are referenced ONLY
!     on this page -- the volume OCR was grepped for 220120, 220124,
!     220135 and 220140 and no card on any other page names them --
!     so nothing in this repository could ever catch a misreading of
!     those four.  Measured by eye on the rendered glyphs:
!
!       * the "1" in "R 1" (line 5), in "R10" (line 34) and in
!         "RS1 FLK" (line 43) has a DIAGONAL FLAG at the upper left
!         and a wide base serif;
!       * the "1"s in "CM1111(" (lines 13, 35, 44) -- which are
!         CONNECTIVE DIGITS and cannot be letters -- print the same
!         glyph;
!       * the "I" in "SIC" (lines 20, 27, 47) and in "LVI" (lines 5,
!         7, 9, 11, 17, 36, 46) is a PLAIN VERTICAL BAR with
!         symmetric serifs and no flag.
!
!     So all three names carry the digit.  RS1 FLK is corroborated
!     off-page: decks/mcp_bootstrap_p151.strap records that C22-6728
!     typesets it "RS1FLK", and p.151's own line 37 branches to
!     220143.10 00 -- this page's line 43.
!
! (4) "R10"'s LAST GLYPH IS ROUND, and this is the weakest reading
!     on the page.  It prints OPEN.  Two things point at a digit
!     zero and neither is conclusive: the R-family is numeric
!     (R 1, R 2, R 9 SKP, R10), and on this page the LETTER O prints
!     CLOSED in "CNOP" (line 31) three rows away while the DIGIT
!     ZERO -- proven a zero by the assembler, see (1) -- prints OPEN
!     in "LX,0" (line 32), "SX,0" (line 33) and "CTL,0.0" (line 34),
!     i.e. in the three rows immediately below it.  But
!     docs/transcription_workflow.md is explicit that openness is
!     not a reliable O/0 discriminator (printed p.85 line 12 prints
!     a clean closed O beside an open one in ONE ROW), so this is
!     recorded as a reading and flagged in WHAT COULD NOT BE
!     RESOLVED.
!
! (5) "S CH OP" (line 13) -- the H is read off another page, not
!     off this one.  printed p.99 line 22's defining card is
!     "S CH OP SYN(BU,1),0.26  -CHAN OPERATING, CHAN", landed in
!     decks/mcp_bootstrap_p99.strap, and line 13's first group
!     000000.32 is 0o32 = bit 26 exactly.  Address and English
!     agree.
!
! (6) "K DENST" and its comment "-SET UNIT TO INSTALAATION DEN"
!     (line 48).  INSTALAATION is IBM's misspelling, not a misread:
!     the glyphs are clean at 400 dpi and the comment column is
!     English, so a reader who "corrected" it would be editing the
!     page.  Recorded verbatim.  K DENST itself is landed by
!     decks/mcp_bootstrap_p154.strap and p156 at (8)217674.40, and
!     line 48 prints 217674.50 00 -- bit 18 set, so this page checks
!     the HALF WORD against them.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! EXTERNALS -- every one pinned, and which bits are OBSERVED
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! READ THIS BEFORE COUNTING A PASSING !CHECK AS A READING.  Three
! different operation classes print three different amounts of an
! operand's address on this page:
!
!   * A VFL OR BRANCH-ON-BIT OPERAND PRINTS ITS FULL 24-BIT BIT
!     ADDRESS.  Lines 13, 26, 35, 43 and 44 therefore check their
!     operand's offset TO THE BIT.
!   * A SIC PRINTS THE BARE 24-BIT ADDRESS (measured in
!     decks/mcp_bootstrap_p160.strap, "THE SIC MEASUREMENT").  Lines
!     20, 27 and 47 check their operand to all six octal digits.
!   * AN INDEX INSTRUCTION PRINTS ONLY BIT 18 OF ITS OPERAND,
!     BECAUSE BITS 19-23 CARRY THE REGISTER.  Measured on this
!     page's own rows, everything else fixed: "SV,0,..." prints .41
!     and "LV,$1,..." prints .42 against the SAME operand S CTL OP
!     (lines 18 and 29), and "LVI,1" prints .03 where "LVI,3" prints
!     .07 and "LVI,$0" prints .01 (lines 36, 17, 5).  The field is
!     bit18*0o40 + register, so lines 5, 7, 9, 11, 14, 17, 18, 19,
!     24, 29, 30, 32, 33, 36 and 46 observe their operand's WORD and
!     bit 18 and nothing below it.
!
!   * A BRANCH PRINTS BIT 18 ONLY (docs/transcription_workflow.md,
!     "WHAT THE ORACLE CANNOT SEE" 1).
!
! S CH OP    0.26       printed p.99 line 22's defining card,
!                       "-CHAN OPERATING, CHAN", SYN(BU,1); landed
!                       in decks/mcp_bootstrap_p99.strap.  Line 13
!                       is a VFL, so its 000000.32 checks bit 26
!                       exactly.
SCHOP   SYN(BU,1), 0.26
! S TIC      215265.00  STored Instruction Counter; twenty decks
!                       carry it at this word and C22-6728 says
!                       "the saved IC is stored in STIC"
!                       (docs/transcription_workflow.md 2b rung 2).
!                       Line 14 is an INDEX instruction and prints
!                       215265.05 -- the .05 is register 2 and bit
!                       18 is 0, so the offset below bit 18 is
!                       unobservable here and the ".0" is the
!                       repository's convention.
STIC    SYN, (8)215265.0
! R MASK     221153.00  DEFINED BY A CARD ON A PAGE NOBODY HAS
!                       OPENED: the volume OCR of this listing
!                       carries, at line 9399, "221153.00
!                       030000.00+  R MASK  VF,(8)3000.  -SPACE
!                       MASK - SEL, VER", one of a run of mask
!                       constants (Z EVL MK at 221152.00, another
!                       at 221152.40, one more at 221153.40).  That
!                       is an INDEX of where the symbol is defined,
!                       on a DIFFERENT page, which rule 5 permits;
!                       it is not corroboration of this page.  THE
!                       PRINTED PAGE IS NOT PINNED: the page-header
!                       OCR between volume-OCR lines 9130 (printed
!                       p.178) and 9380 is unreadable, so the card
!                       is somewhere in printed pp.179-184 and this
!                       deck does not guess which.  Line 17 is an
!                       LVI, so this page observes the word and bit
!                       18 (which is 0) and not the offset.
RMASK   SYN, (8)221153.0
! S CTL OP   5.42       printed p.101 line 19's defining card,
!                       "-OCTAL CONTROL OP CODE", SYN(BU,8); landed
!                       in decks/mcp_bootstrap_p101.strap.  LINE 26
!                       PINS IT TO THE BIT: a BB operand prints its
!                       full bit address, and "S CTL OP+.1" prints
!                       000005.53 = 0o53 = bit 43 = 42 + 1.  Lines
!                       18 and 29 are index instructions and see
!                       only that bit 18 is set.
SCTLOP  SYN(BU,8), 5.42
! S EVL MK   2.32       printed p.101 line 12's defining card,
!                       "-EVALUATION MASK", SYN(BU,19).  Line 19 is
!                       an index instruction: 000002.47 observes
!                       word 2 and bit 18 set, which is consistent
!                       with 32 but does not measure it.
SEVLMK  SYN(BU,19), 2.32
! S EVL TB   2.0        printed p.101 line 11's defining card,
!                       "-EVALUATION TABLE BASE", SYN(BU,19).  Line
!                       20 is a SIC, so all six octal digits are
!                       checked: 000002.00 8C.
SEVLTB  SYN(BU,19), 2.0
! R VFY      221161.00  ITS OWN LABEL ROW IS PRINTED LATER IN THE
!                       VOLUME: volume OCR line 9419 reads
!                       "221161.00  000007.31 8C  221165.74 02
!                       R VFY  BZB,S SYSTM($12),R RR  -TEST IF
!                       SYSTEM TAPE".  An index of another page, and
!                       the word agrees with this page's line 23.
!                       Also landed as an external by
!                       decks/mcp_bootstrap_p151.strap and p153.
!                       Line 23 is a branch, so bit 18 only (it is
!                       0).
RVFY    SYN, (8)221161.0
! S FILEK    7.38       printed p.101 line 25's defining card,
!                       "-FILE COUNT", SYN(BU,12).  THE SPELLING IS
!                       THE PAGE'S OWN AND IT IS PRINTED BOTH WAYS
!                       HERE: line 24's statement prints "S FILEK"
!                       and line 40's comment prints "S FILE K", one
!                       page, one assembler -- which is IBM telling
!                       us the blank is not part of the name.  Line
!                       24 is an index instruction (000007.52), so
!                       word 7 and bit 18 set are observed and the
!                       38 comes from p.101.
SFILEK  SYN(BU,12), 7.38
! S SCR PM   6.0        printed p.101 line 20's defining card,
!                       "-SCRATCH PAD MEMORY", SYN(BU,64).  Line 27
!                       is a SIC: 000006.00 8C checks all six octal
!                       digits.
SSCRPM  SYN(BU,64), 6.0
! S IO INS   4.0        printed p.101 line 17's defining card,
!                       "-IO INSTR. LAST", SYN(BU,64).  Line 33 is
!                       an index instruction (000004.01) -- word 4,
!                       bit 18 clear.
SIOINS  SYN(BU,64), 4.0
! S CTL      0.60       printed p.99 line 48's defining card,
!                       "-CONTROL - OPERATION", SYN(BU,1).  Lines 35
!                       and 44 are VFL and print 000000.74 = bit 60
!                       exactly -- twice.
SCTL    SYN(BU,1), 0.60
! K SUPP     216242.00  printed p.128's label row "K SUPP NOP,K COMM
!                       R", as nineteen other decks write it.  Line
!                       36 is an LVI: 216242.03, bit 18 clear, and
!                       the .03 is the register-1 field.
KSUPP   SYN, (8)216242.0
! M IOREJ    221261.00  carried at this word by six landed decks.
!                       Line 37 is a branch: 221261.10 00, bit 18
!                       clear.
MIOREJ  SYN, (8)221261.0
! S SYSTM    7.25       printed p.101 line 23's defining card,
!                       "-SYSTEM TAPE FLAG", SYN(BU,1).  Line 43 is
!                       a BZB, so its 000007.31 = 0o31 = bit 25
!                       checks the offset exactly.
SSYSTM  SYN(BU,1), 7.25
! K N SYST   220174.40  DEFINED ON THE VERY NEXT PAGE.  Volume OCR
!                       line 8439 reads "220174.40  000000.01 01
!                       K N SYST  LVI,0,1.0" -- a label row on
!                       printed p.163, which is an index of ANOTHER
!                       page and so admissible.  Line 43's second
!                       group 220174.74 00 has bit 18 SET, which is
!                       this page's own reading of the .40 and
!                       agrees with it; the bits below 18 come from
!                       p.163 when it lands.
KNSYST  SYN, (8)220174.40
! K SP TM    220156.40  ALSO ON THE VERY NEXT PAGE.  Volume OCR line
!                       8418: "220156.40  000002.33 8D  002127.00 50
!                       K SP TM  L(B,2,1),S FCEN($12),46  -SPACE
!                       OVER TAPE MARK".  Line 45's second group
!                       220156.74 00 carries bit 18 set, agreeing.
KSPTM   SYN, (8)220156.40
! S INST D   0.0        printed p.108 line 6's defining card,
!                       "-INSTALLATION DENSITY", SYN(BU,24); landed
!                       in decks/mcp_bootstrap_p108.strap.  Line 46
!                       is an LVI (000000.01), word 0 and bit 18
!                       clear.
SINSTD  SYN(BU,24), 0.0
! S RET AD   5.0        printed p.101 line 18's defining card,
!                       "-RETURN ADDRESS IN MCP", SYN(BU,19).  Line
!                       47 is a SIC: 000005.00 8C, all six digits.
SRETAD  SYN(BU,19), 5.0
! K DENST    217674.40  landed by decks/mcp_bootstrap_p154.strap and
!                       p156.  Line 48 prints 217674.50 00, bit 18
!                       SET, so this page checks the half word.
KDENST  SYN, (8)217674.40
! Z STEVL    221144.40  printed p.160's deck renders its label row
!                       first hand at 900 dpi.  Line 21 prints
!                       221144.50 00, bit 18 set, agreeing.
ZSTEVL  SYN, (8)221144.40
! K K LOC    220065.40  printed p.160's OWN LABEL ROW (line 11,
!                       rendered there at 900 dpi).  Line 22 prints
!                       220065.50 00, bit 18 set, agreeing.
KKLOC   SYN, (8)220065.40
! K ILEGL    220064.00  printed p.160's OWN LABEL ROW (line 8).
!                       Line 16 prints 220064.32 C0 -- byte for byte
!                       printed p.159 line 6's card and printed
!                       p.161 line 26's card as well (volume OCR,
!                       index) -- and line 26's second group prints
!                       220064.34 02.  Bit 18 is clear in both, so
!                       this page checks the half word TWICE against
!                       p.160's label row.
KILEGL  SYN, (8)220064.0

! >>> SUBSTITUTION, LABELLED LOUDLY: THE PAGE PRINTS "SLC,$". <<<
! Line 4 is "220120.40  *  SLC,$".  A hard SLC cannot disagree with
! the arriving counter -- it overrides it silently
! (docs/transcription_workflow.md, "WHAT THE ORACLE CANNOT SEE" 2) --
! so writing the origin here ASSERTS 220120.40 and throws away the
! page's own free witness.  THE PRINTED LOCATION IS RECORDED HERE
! BECAUSE NO !CHECK CAN USE IT: 220120.40, and the volume OCR of
! printed p.161 (line 8346, an index of ANOTHER page) shows that
! page's last located row as "220120.00  220017.50 00  B,..." -- a
! HALF WORD at 220120.00, which leaves the counter at exactly
! 220120.40.  When p.161 lands, compare the two by hand.
        SLC, (8)220120.40            ! line  4  printed "SLC,$"

R1      LVI,$0,62.0                  ! line  5
        B,R2                         ! line  6  same card as 5
        LVI,$0,126.0                 ! line  7
        B,R2                         ! line  8  same card as 7
        LVI,$0,63.0                  ! line  9
        B,R2                         ! line 10  same card as 9
        LVI,$0,127.0                 ! line 11
R2      V-I,2,.32                    ! line 12
        CM1111(BU,1),SCHOP($10)      ! line 13  printed "S CH OP"
        SV,2,STIC                    ! line 14  printed "S TIC"
        KVI,4,4.0                    ! line 15
